<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#tui-haatakalaua">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tuʻi Haʻatakalaua</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tu'i Ha'atakalaua</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tuʻi Haʻatakalaua was the second line of Tongan kings, created in the 15th century after the assassination of the Tuʻi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tonga">Tonga</a> Takalaua. His son Kauʻulufonua I avenged him and gave his brother Moʻungāmotuʻa authority over everyday government, while the Tuʻi Tonga kept sacred rank. In the 17th century a junior branch of this line, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tui-kanokupolu">Tuʻi Kanokupolu</a>, took over its executive role, and from it descends the present royal house.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The first holder, Moʻungāmotuʻa, appears around 1470. His elder brother Kauʻulufonua I, the Tuʻi Tonga, stayed in his residence on the high ground of Olotele at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mua-tonga">Muʻa</a>, while Moʻungāmotuʻa lived on the low land of Fonuamotu, reclaimed from the lagoon, the two separated by the Fonuamoa road. His followers therefore became known as the Kauhalalalo, the low road people, and the chiefs of the Tuʻi Tonga line as the Kauhalaʻuta, the inland road people. Later events suggest that Moʻungāmotuʻa took power from his brother without daring to do away with the Tuʻi Tonga, sending Kauʻulufonua to Samoa and ruling in his name; it took about a century for the new line to eclipse the old.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Around 1550 Vakalahi-Moheʻuli, or his father, allowed the Tuʻi Tonga to return from exile in Samoa. Moʻunga ʻo Tonga appointed several sons as governors, among them Ngata in the Hihifo district, who began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tui-kanokupolu">Tuʻi Kanokupolu</a> line, and a daughter married the Tuʻi Tonga Fatafehi, joining the two dynasties by blood. Fotofili met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abel-tasman">Abel Tasman</a> in 1643. Vaea found that the Tuʻi Kanokupolu had become a serious rival and fought a civil war against Mataelehaʻamea.</p><p>After him quick successions point to decline. Tupoulahi, who gave up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tui-kanokupolu">Tuʻi Kanokupolu</a> title around 1771 because of old age, may have been offered this title, though it is doubted that he was formally installed. Maealiuaki, also a former Tuʻi Kanokupolu, met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-cook">Captain Cook</a> in 1777 and died soon after, and with him went the last real holder. Toafunaki, named by missionaries around 1790, died young in 1797, and his reburial in 1799 gave the occasion for the assassination of the Tuʻi Kanokupolu Tukuʻaho.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>By the time of Mulikihaʻamea the title had become defunct, but his descendants took a new one, Tungī, still among the highest noble titles in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tonga">Tonga</a>; Viliami Tungī Mailefihi of this line joined it with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tui-kanokupolu">Tuʻi Kanokupolu</a>. Four of Tonga's 33 hereditary noble titles, Tungī, Luani, Fotofili and Fakafānua, trace their authority to the line. Its burial grounds lie on ʻEua and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mua-tonga">Lapaha</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tuʻi Haʻatakalaua” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Tradition relates that after the assassination of Takalaua, probably in the fifteenth century though some reckonings place it in the sixteenth, his son Kauʻulufonua I gave day-to-day authority to his brother Moʻungāmotuʻa, the first <b>Tuʻi Haʻatakalaua</b>, while the Tuʻi Tonga kept supreme rank and sacred functions.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tu-i-tonga-empire">Tuʻi Tonga Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
